description: """\

pulicomv provides pull-based, on-demand incremental computation over
mutable vars: memoized nodes derived from vars via applicative
combinators, recomputed lazily on read against a shared logical clock,
with a per-node cutoff. The main module and entry point of the library
is named [Cache].

It started from a question: what do you end up with if you're ready to
give up on [bind]? Without it, a node's parents are fixed when it is
built, and the machinery a self-adjusting-computation engine needs to
order its recomputations — heights, a priority queue, a scheduler — has
nothing left to do.

What remains is two passes, neither of them a stabilization. A write
([Var.set]) marks the nodes downstream of it as possibly stale and
recomputes nothing; the marking disconnects each node it reaches from
its parents as it goes, so it only ever walks what has been read back
since the last write, and a burst of writes with no read in between
costs essentially nothing after the first. A read ([Node.value]) is
what checks: it refreshes the node's own parents, reconnecting to them,
and re-fires its closure only if one of them really moved.

pulicomv borrows ideas and terminology from Jane Street's [incremental],
which remains the library to reach for when [bind] is what you need.
See the project's NOTICE.md for details.

[incremental]: https://github.com/janestreet/incremental

"""
tags: [ "incremental" "cache" "memoization" ]
